{"text":"Lets each position mix in information from itself and earlier positions, weighted by how well its query matches their keys. The per-token vector that runs through the whole model. Every layer reads it and adds its output back instead of replacing it. While generating, the keys and values of past tokens are stored so each new token only computes its own. It grows with every token and every layer. Generating with a small draft model that guesses several tokens and a large model that checks them all in one pass, keeping the agreeing prefix; the output is the same as the large model’s. Storing numbers in fewer bits, for example weights as 8- or 4-bit integers with a scale, to make a model smaller and faster to serve at a small cost in accuracy. For every weight, how fast the loss changes as that weight changes. Training moves each weight a little against its gradient. Computing the gradient of the loss for every weight at once, by applying the chain rule layer by layer from the output back to the input. Training a pretrained model a little further, on a smaller dataset for one task, usually with a small new output layer on top. A model doing a task it is shown in its prompt, by examples or instructions, with no change to its weights. Text a model writes to ask the surrounding program to run a function, such as JSON with a name and arguments; the program runs it and puts the result back in the context.","tokens":298,"terms":["Attention","Residual stream","KV cache","Speculative decoding","Quantization","Gradient","Backpropagation","Fine-tuning","In-context learning","Tool call"],"models":[{"name":"GPT-2 small","layers":12,"width":768,"params":124318464,"nonEmbedding":84934656,"loss":4.358,"losses":[8.129,10.03,8.346,10.16,2.492,8.067,2.414,11.02,1.904,12.34,6.053,2.723,10,0.605,4.081,3.019,5.059,8.167,3.347,5.027,9.18,0.4697,2.612,9.459,0.513,7.874,7.894,5.572,6.398,1.885,1.592,5.285,5.781,4.659,6.404,6.906,7.767,3.734,1.602,3.784,2.52,4.792,4.318,8.585,0.4458,4